1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,712
Chapter 9: Preconceptional Counseling.

3
00:00:05,712 --> 00:00:23,375
Counseling Session Medical History Genetic Diseases Reproductive History Parental Age Social History Screening Tests References The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) defines preconceptional health to encompass the overall health of nonpregnant women during their reproductive years .

4
00:00:23,375 --> 00:00:46,150
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) and the Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2014, 2021) attest to the importance of preconceptional care and promote the following objectives to advance it: 1. Improve knowledge, attitudes, and behaviors of men and women related to preconceptional health 2.

5
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:59,512
Assure that all childbearing-aged women receive preconceptional care services—including evidence-based risk screening, health promotion, and interventions—that will enable them to enter pregnancy in optimal health 3.

6
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:05,862
Implement interconceptional interventions to prevent or minimize recurrent adverse outcomes 4.

7
00:01:05,862 --> 00:01:12,675
Reduce the racial and socioeconomical disparities in adverse pregnancy outcomes Table 9-1.

8
00:01:12,675 --> 00:01:18,325
lists the prevalence of many conditions often amenable to preconceptional intervention .

9
00:01:18,325 --> 00:01:26,237
These are frequently encountered by generalist obstetrician–gynecologists, who can help optimize health entering pregnancy .

10
00:01:26,237 --> 00:01:36,150
For example, by the time most women realize they are pregnant—usually 1 to 2 weeks after the first missed period—the embryo has already begun to form.

11
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:44,662
Thus, many preventive steps —such as folic acid to avoid neural-tube defects—will be ineffective if initiated at this time.

12
00:01:44,662 --> 00:01:52,362
Moreover, 45 percent of all pregnancies in the United States are unplanned, and often these are at greatest risk .

13
00:01:52,362 --> 00:02:00,350
Last, a disproportionate number of indigent women receive less preconceptional care compared with their more affluent counterparts .

14
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:02,612
TABLE 9-1.

15
00:02:02,612 --> 00:02:16,312
Preconceptional Health Indicators—United States, 2013–2015 Few randomized trials evaluate preconceptional care efficacy, in part because withholding such counseling would be unethical.

16
00:02:16,312 --> 00:02:24,175
Also, pregnancy outcomes are dependent on the interaction of various maternal, fetal, and environmental factors.

17
00:02:24,175 --> 00:02:33,612
Thus, ascribing a salutary outcome to a specific intervention is difficult (Temel, 2014; Tieu, 2017).

18
00:02:33,612 --> 00:02:41,375
However, prospective observational and case-control studies have demonstrated the successes of preconceptional counseling .

19
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:45,737
Therefore, routine pregnancy intention screening should be done .

20
00:02:45,737 --> 00:02:47,750
Counseling Session.

21
00:02:47,750 --> 00:03:00,000
Obstetricians–gynecologists, internists, family practitioners, and pediatricians have the best opportunity to provide preventive counseling during periodic health maintenance examinations.

22
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,512
The occasion of a negative pregnancy test is also an excellent time for education .

23
00:03:05,512 --> 00:03:19,175
Jack and colleagues (1995) administered a comprehensive preconceptional risk survey to 136 such women, and almost 95 percent reported at least one problem that could affect a future pregnancy .

24
00:03:19,175 --> 00:03:32,412
Providers should be knowledgeable regarding relevant medical diseases, prior surgery, reproductive disorders, or genetic conditions and be able to interpret data and recommendations provided by other specialists .

25
00:03:32,412 --> 00:03:43,075
Women presenting specifically for preconceptional evaluation are advised that information collection may be time consuming, depending on the number and complexity of factors.

26
00:03:43,075 --> 00:03:50,412
The intake evaluation includes a thorough review of the medical, obstetrical, social, and family histories.

27
00:03:50,412 --> 00:04:01,750
Useful information is more likely to be obtained by asking specific questions regarding each of these histories and the health of each family member than by asking general, open-ended questions.

28
00:04:01,750 --> 00:04:07,225
Some important information can be obtained by questionnaires that address these topics.

29
00:04:07,225 --> 00:04:14,162
Answers are reviewed with the couple to ensure appropriate follow-up, including obtaining relevant medical records.

30
00:04:14,162 --> 00:04:16,812
# The Fourth Trimester.

31
00:04:16,812 --> 00:04:22,575
Another optimal time to begin preconceptional counseling is during the “fourth trimester.

32
00:04:22,575 --> 00:04:28,312
This was emphasized by the Presidential Task Force on Redefining the Postpartum Visit .

33
00:04:28,312 --> 00:04:32,187
Designed to optimize postpartum care and provide

34
00:04:32,356 --> 00:04:39,756
contraceptive counseling, it also sets the stage for any subsequent pregnancy and for the woman’s long-term health.

35
00:04:39,706 --> 00:04:54,606
To assist the provider, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) has joined the Society of Maternal–Fetal Medicine to provide an Obstetric Care Consensus guide that emphasizes the interpregnancy period.

36
00:04:54,606 --> 00:04:56,556
Medical History.

37
00:04:56,556 --> 00:05:05,956
With specific medical conditions, general points include the pregnancy’s effect on the condition and the disorder’s influence on the fetus and pregnancy course.

38
00:05:05,956 --> 00:05:32,331
Some chronic conditions that may worsen pregnancy outcomes include treated or active cancer, prior peripartum cardiomyopathy, antiphospholipid antibodies, systemic lupus erythematosus, and congenital heart disease (Amant, 2015; Cunningham, 2019; Davis, 2021; Foeller, 2018; Gibbins, 2018; Hopkins, 2018).

39
00:05:32,331 --> 00:05:39,843
Importantly, psychological health also is considered (Barker, 2020; Lassi, 2014).

40
00:05:39,843 --> 00:05:49,381
Detailed preconceptional information regarding a few exemplary conditions is found in the next sections and in the other topic-specific chapters of this text.

41
00:05:49,381 --> 00:05:52,193
# Diabetes Mellitus.

42
00:05:52,193 --> 00:06:02,843
Because maternal and fetal pathology associated with hyperglycemia is well known, diabetes is the prototype of a condition for which preconceptional counseling is beneficial.

43
00:06:02,843 --> 00:06:10,956
Diabetes-associated risks to both mother and fetus are discussed in detail in Chapter 60 (p. 1068).

44
00:06:10,956 --> 00:06:18,356
If a patient maintains glucose levels close to normal, many of these complications can be avoided before conception.

45
00:06:18,356 --> 00:06:27,356
Another important aspect of counseling pertains to the frequent use of teratogenic angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors in this population .

46
00:06:27,356 --> 00:06:40,806
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018f) has concluded that preconceptional counseling for women with pregestational diabetes is beneficial and cost-effective and should be encouraged.

47
00:06:40,806 --> 00:06:50,668
The American Diabetes Association (2004) has promulgated consensus recommendations for preconceptional care for women with diabetes.

48
00:06:50,668 --> 00:06:59,943
These guidelines advise an inventory of disease duration and related complications and clinical and laboratory examination for end-organ damage.

49
00:06:59,943 --> 00:07:07,368
Perhaps most essential, they encourage a preconceptional hemoglobin A1c level goal below 7 percent.

50
00:07:07,368 --> 00:07:20,931
In addition to assessing diabetic control, hemoglobin A1c measurement can also forecast the risks for gestational diabetes and for major anomalies (Hinkle, 2018; Martin, 2020).

51
00:07:20,931 --> 00:07:23,218
Figure 9-1.

52
00:07:23,218 --> 00:07:34,381
Relationship between first-trimester glycosylated hemoglobin values and risk for major congenital malformations in 320 women with insulin-dependent diabetes.

53
00:07:34,381 --> 00:07:44,018
Although no randomized trials attest to the success of preconceptional counseling in women with diabetes, cohort studies do demonstrate its effectiveness .

54
00:07:44,018 --> 00:07:56,831
In a prospective study of 5075 affected women, preconceptional counseling improved hemoglobin A1c levels, folic acid compliance, and “optimal” pregnancy preparation .

55
00:07:56,831 --> 00:08:11,543
Diabetic women who undergo preconceptional counseling also have improved glycemic control before pregnancy and in the first trimester and experience lower rates of adverse outcomes—defined as a perinatal death or major congenital anomaly .

56
00:08:11,543 --> 00:08:22,256
Despite such benefits, in one study of approximately 300 diabetic women in a managed-care plan, only approximately half received preconceptional counseling .

57
00:08:22,256 --> 00:08:24,531
# Epilepsy.

58
00:08:24,531 --> 00:08:32,318
Compared with unaffected women, those with a seizure disorder carry an augmented risk of neonates with structural anomalies.

59
00:08:32,318 --> 00:08:42,493
Some early reports indicated that epilepsy conferred an elevated a priori risk for congenital malformations that was independent of anticonvulsant treatment effects.

60
00:08:42,493 --> 00:08:57,368
More recent studies have largely failed to confirm this, but it is difficult to refute entirely because women who are controlled without medication generally have less severe disease (Cassina, 2013; Vajda, 2015).

61
00:08:57,368 --> 00:09:03,156
Polytherapy is associated with a higher malformation risk compared with monotherapy .

62
00:09:03,156 --> 00:09:15,031
Last, in women using antiseizure medications, the risks for miscarriage and stillbirth in do not appear elevated (Aghajanian, 2015; Bech, 2014).

63
00:09:15,031 --> 00:09:19,481
Ideally, medications are adjusted preconceptionally to

64
00:09:19,636 --> 00:09:22,198
minimize seizure frequency.

65
00:09:22,148 --> 00:09:34,786
From one national registry, the seizure risk during pregnancy was 50 to 70 percent lower in women without a seizure in the year preceding pregnancy compared with a group experiencing seizures in this preceding year .

66
00:09:34,786 --> 00:09:39,561
No further advantages accrued if the seizure-free period exceeded a year.

67
00:09:39,561 --> 00:09:50,873
Treatment goals attempt to control seizure frequency with monotherapy and with medications considered less teratogenic (Aguglia, 2009; Tomson, 2009).

68
00:09:50,873 --> 00:10:00,736
Discussed in Chapter 63 (p. 1129) and shown in Table 9-2, some onedrug regimens are more teratogenic than others.

69
00:10:00,736 --> 00:10:15,973
In particular, valproic acid is avoided if possible, as it has consistently been associated with a greater risk for major congenital malformations than other antiepileptic drugs (Jentink, 2010; Vajda, 2015).

70
00:10:15,973 --> 00:10:21,473
Information concerning the teratogenicity of newer antiepileptics is limited .

71
00:10:21,473 --> 00:10:23,723
Table 9-2.

72
00:10:23,723 --> 00:10:38,098
First-Trimester Antiepileptic Monotherapy and the Associated Major Malformation Risk The American Academy of Neurology recommends consideration of antiseizure medication discontinuation before pregnancy in suitable candidates .

73
00:10:38,098 --> 00:10:56,911
These include women who satisfy the following criteria: (1) have been seizure-free for 2 to 5 years, (2) display a single seizure type, (3) have a normal neurological examination and normal intelligence, and (4) show electroencephalogram results that have normalized with treatment.

74
00:10:56,911 --> 00:11:03,086
Women with seizures should be advised to take a daily 4-mg oral folic acid supplement.

75
00:11:03,086 --> 00:11:12,011
Even so, the value of folate to reduce fetal malformation rates in pregnant women taking anticonvulsant therapy is not entirely clear.

76
00:11:12,011 --> 00:11:26,436
In one case-control study, the congenital abnormality risk was reduced by maternal folate supplementation in fetuses exposed to carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, and primidone (Kjær, 2008).

77
00:11:26,436 --> 00:11:38,461
Conversely, Morrow and coworkers (2009) compared fetal outcomes of women who received preconceptional folic acid with those who did not receive it until later in pregnancy or not at all.

78
00:11:38,461 --> 00:11:47,511
In this study, a paradoxical increase in the number of major congenital malformations was observed in the group who received preconceptional folate.

79
00:11:47,511 --> 00:11:57,073
These investigators concluded that folate metabolism may be only a part of the mechanism by which malformations are induced in women taking these medications.

80
00:11:57,073 --> 00:11:59,586
# Immunizations.

81
00:11:59,586 --> 00:12:05,236
Preconceptional counseling includes assessment of immunity against common pathogens.

82
00:12:05,236 --> 00:12:15,861
Also, depending on health status, travel plans, and time of year, other immunizations may be indicated as discussed in Chapter 10 (p. 189).

83
00:12:15,861 --> 00:12:20,673
Several immunization resources are listed in Table 9-3.

84
00:12:20,673 --> 00:12:26,536
Vaccines that contain toxoids such as tetanus are suitable before or during gestation.

85
00:12:26,536 --> 00:12:43,273
Also, those containing killed bacteria or viruses—such as influenza, pneumococcus, hepatitis B, meningococcus, and rabies vaccines—are not associated with adverse fetal outcomes and are not contraindicated preconceptionally or during pregnancy.

86
00:12:43,273 --> 00:12:48,273
Conversely, live-virus vaccines are not recommended during pregnancy.

87
00:12:48,273 --> 00:12:56,586
Examples are vaccines against varicella-zoster, measles, mumps, rubella, polio, chickenpox, and yellow fever.

88
00:12:56,586 --> 00:13:03,023
Moreover, 1 month or longer should ideally pass between vaccination and conception attempts.

89
00:13:03,023 --> 00:13:15,448
That said, inadvertent administration of measles, mumps, rubella (MMR) or varicella vaccines during pregnancy should not generally be considered indications for pregnancy termination.

90
00:13:15,448 --> 00:13:19,886
Most reports indicate that the fetal risk is only theoretical.

91
00:13:19,886 --> 00:13:27,373
Immunization to smallpox, anthrax, and other bioterrorism diseases is discussed if clinically appropriate .

92
00:13:27,373 --> 00:13:29,698
Table 9-3.

93
00:13:29,698 --> 00:13:34,848
Immunization Resources With some infections, vaccines are unavailable.

94
00:13:34,848 --> 00:13:38,098
One recent example is the Zika virus .

95
00:13:38,098 --> 00:13:50,261
For this virus, during the 2016 epidemic, the CDC issued travel advisories for pregnant women (Petersen, 2016; SchulerFaccini, 2016).

96
00:13:50,261 --> 00:13:52,386
Genetic Diseases.

97
00:13:52,386 --> 00:14:01,586
The CDC (2016) estimates that 3 percent of neonates born each year in the United States will have at least one birth defect.

98
00:14:01,586 --> 00:14:08,611
Importantly, such defects are the leading cause of infant mortality and account for 20 percent of deaths.

99
00:14:08,611 --> 00:14:10,636
Ethics preclude

100
00:14:10,804 --> 00:14:15,491
randomized trials of preconceptional counseling for genetic risk .

101
00:14:15,441 --> 00:14:25,354
Instead, the benefits of preconceptional counseling usually are measured by comparing the incidence of new cases before and after initiation of such a program.

102
00:14:25,354 --> 00:14:39,829
Specific congenital conditions that clearly benefit from patient education include neural-tube defects, phenylketonuria, thalassemias, and other genetic diseases more common in individuals of Eastern European Jewish descent .

103
00:14:39,829 --> 00:14:52,991
Other missed opportunities for genetic consultation were found for women with a personal or family history of birth defects, intellectual disability or autism, and a prior positive genetic carrier screening test .

104
00:14:52,991 --> 00:14:55,529
# Family History.

105
00:14:55,529 --> 00:15:04,704
Pedigree construction using the symbols shown in Figure 9-2 is the most thorough method for obtaining a family history as a part of genetic screening.

106
00:15:04,704 --> 00:15:15,954
The health and reproductive status of each “blood relative” are individually reviewed for medical illnesses, mental retardation, birth defects, infertility, and pregnancy loss.

107
00:15:15,954 --> 00:15:23,491
Certain racial, ethnic, or religious backgrounds may indicate elevated risk for specific recessive disorders.

108
00:15:23,491 --> 00:15:25,779
Figure 9-2.

109
00:15:25,779 --> 00:15:28,841
Symbols used for pedigree construction.

110
00:15:28,841 --> 00:15:35,216
Although most women can provide information regarding their history, their understanding may be limited.

111
00:15:35,216 --> 00:15:41,416
Pregnant women may fail to communicate a birth defect in the family or may report it incorrectly.

112
00:15:41,416 --> 00:15:52,029
Thus, any disclosed defect or genetic disease is ideally confirmed by reviewing pertinent medical records or by contacting affected relatives for additional information.

113
00:15:52,029 --> 00:15:54,804
# Neural-Tube Defects.

114
00:15:54,804 --> 00:16:07,141
The incidence of neural-tube defects (NTDs) is 0.9 per 1000 live births, and they are second only to cardiac anomalies as the most frequent structural fetal malformation.

115
00:16:07,141 --> 00:16:11,904
Some of these malformations are associated with specific mutations.

116
00:16:11,904 --> 00:16:19,866
One example is the 677C → T substitution in the gene that encodes methylene tetrahydrofolate reductase.

117
00:16:19,866 --> 00:16:31,354
For this and similar gene defects, one seminal trial showed that preconceptional folic acid therapy significantly reduced the risk for a recurrent NTD by 72 percent .

118
00:16:31,354 --> 00:16:45,791
More importantly, because more than 90 percent of neonates with NTDs are born to women at low risk, Czeizel and Dudas (1992) showed that universal supplementation reduced the a priori risk of a first NTD.

119
00:16:45,791 --> 00:17:01,091
Because of these findings, all women who may become pregnant are recommended to take daily 400 to 800 μg of folic acid orally before conception and through the first trimester (U.S. Preventive Services Task Force, 2019).

120
00:17:01,091 --> 00:17:10,966
Folate fortification of cereal grains has been mandatory in the United States since 1998, and this practice has lowered neural-tube defect rates .

121
00:17:10,966 --> 00:17:24,354
Despite the demonstrated benefits of folate supplementation, only half of women take folic acid supplementation periconceptionally (de Jong-van den Berg, 2005; Goldberg, 2006).

122
00:17:24,354 --> 00:17:26,979
# Phenylketonuria.

123
00:17:26,979 --> 00:17:32,866
More than 600 mutations have been identified in the phenylalanine hydroxylase gene.

124
00:17:32,866 --> 00:17:43,341
The inherited defect in phenylalanine metabolism exemplifies diseases in which the fetus may not be at risk to inherit the disorder but may be damaged by maternal disease.

125
00:17:43,341 --> 00:17:52,191
Specifically, mothers with phenylketonuria (PKU) who eat an unrestricted diet have abnormally high blood phenylalanine levels.

126
00:17:52,191 --> 00:18:00,741
This amino acid readily crosses the placenta and can damage developing fetal organs, especially neural and cardiac tissues.

127
00:18:00,741 --> 00:18:14,716
With appropriate preconceptional counseling and adherence to a phenylalanine-restricted diet before pregnancy, the incidence of fetal malformations is dramatically reduced (Camp, 2014; Vockley, 2014).

128
00:18:14,716 --> 00:18:23,929
Therefore, the phenylalanine concentration is ideally brought into normal range 3 months before conception and then maintained there throughout pregnancy .

129
00:18:23,929 --> 00:18:30,466
The target phenylalanine blood concentration is 120 to 360 µmol/L .

130
00:18:30,466 --> 00:18:32,954
# Thalassemias.

131
00:18:32,954 --> 00:18:43,329
These disorders of globin-chain synthesis are the most common single-gene disorders worldwide (Forget, 2013; Vichinsky, 2013).

132
00:18:43,329 --> 00:18:52,866
As many as 200 million people carry a gene for one of these hemoglobinopathies, and hundreds of mutations are known to cause thalassemia syndromes .

133
00:18:52,866 --> 00:18:54,604
In endemic

134
00:18:54,772 --> 00:19:04,984
areas such as Mediterranean and Southeast Asian countries, counseling and other prevention strategies have reduced the incidence of new cases by up to 80 percent .

135
00:19:04,934 --> 00:19:19,934
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) recommends that individuals of high-risk ancestry be offered carrier screening to allow them informed decision-making regarding reproduction and prenatal diagnosis.

136
00:19:19,934 --> 00:19:31,084
One method of early prenatal diagnosis for some thalassemia syndromes is preimplantation genetic testing (PGT), which is coupled with assisted reproductive technology .

137
00:19:31,084 --> 00:19:35,297
# Individuals of Eastern European Jewish Descent.

138
00:19:35,297 --> 00:19:46,772
Most individuals of Jewish ancestry in North America are descended from Ashkenazi Jewish communities and are at greater risk for having offspring with one of several autosomal recessive disorders.

139
00:19:46,772 --> 00:20:00,984
These include Tay-Sachs disease, Gaucher disease, cystic fibrosis, Canavan disease, familial dysautonomia, mucolipidosis IV, Niemann-Pick disease type A, Fanconi anemia group C, and Bloom syndrome.

140
00:20:00,984 --> 00:20:12,884
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017a; 2019a) recommends preconceptional counseling and screening for these disorders in this population.

141
00:20:12,884 --> 00:20:20,872
Carrier frequency and features of these conditions are discussed in greater detail in Chapter 17 (p. 343).

142
00:20:20,872 --> 00:20:23,109
Reproductive History.

143
00:20:23,109 --> 00:20:43,172
During preconceptional screening, information is sought regarding infertility; abnormal pregnancy outcomes that may include miscarriage, ectopic pregnancy, molar pregnancy, and recurrent pregnancy loss; and obstetrical complications such as cesarean delivery, preeclampsia, placental abruption, and preterm delivery .

144
00:20:43,172 --> 00:20:51,397
As discussed in Chapter 35 (p. 626), details involving a prior stillbirth are especially important.

145
00:20:51,397 --> 00:21:01,759
For example, Korteweg and associates (2008) identified chromosomal abnormalities in 13 percent of stillborns who underwent karyotyping.

146
00:21:01,759 --> 00:21:13,922
And, Reddy and colleagues (2012) confirmed that chromosomal microarray analysis (CMA) yields better detection of genetic abnormalities than does standard karyotyping.

147
00:21:13,922 --> 00:21:19,172
This primarily stems from CMA’s ability to assess nonviable tissue .

148
00:21:19,172 --> 00:21:25,297
Identification of a genetic abnormality in a stillborn can help to determine recurrence risk.

149
00:21:25,297 --> 00:21:27,184
Parental Age.

150
00:21:27,184 --> 00:21:29,634
# Maternal Age.

151
00:21:29,634 --> 00:21:34,972
Women at both ends of the reproductive-age spectrum have unique outcomes to consider.

152
00:21:34,972 --> 00:21:44,284
First, according to the CDC, 3.4 percent of births in the United States in 2010 were in women aged 15 to 19 years .

153
00:21:44,284 --> 00:21:50,334
An international study reported a rate of 11.9 percent for this age group globally .

154
00:21:50,334 --> 00:21:59,622
These adolescents are at higher risk for anemia, preterm delivery, and preeclampsia compared with women aged 20 to 35 years .

155
00:21:59,622 --> 00:22:06,684
The incidence of sexually transmitted diseases—common in adolescents—is even higher during pregnancy .

156
00:22:06,684 --> 00:22:14,309
Unfortunately, because most of their pregnancies are unplanned, adolescents rarely seek preconceptional counseling.

157
00:22:14,309 --> 00:22:22,022
Conceptions after age 35 currently constitute approximately 15 percent of pregnancies in the United States .

158
00:22:22,022 --> 00:22:27,409
By contrast, these women are more likely to request preconceptional counseling.

159
00:22:27,409 --> 00:22:34,509
Motivations may stem from a desire to optimize outcomes at their age or with infertility treatment or both.

160
00:22:34,509 --> 00:22:40,472
Some studies—including data from Parkland Hospital presented in Figure 9-3.

161
00:22:40,472 --> 00:22:50,809
indicate that risks for obstetrical complications and for perinatal morbidity and mortality rise after age 35 (Waldenström, 2015).

162
00:22:50,809 --> 00:22:57,834
The older woman who has a chronic illness or who is in poor physical condition usually has readily apparent risks.

163
00:22:57,834 --> 00:23:03,334
For the physically fit woman without medical problems, however, the risks are lower.

164
00:23:03,334 --> 00:23:05,672
Figure 9-3.

165
00:23:05,672 --> 00:23:16,059
Incidence of selected pregnancy complications in relation to maternal age among 295,667 women delivered at Parkland Hospital.

166
00:23:16,059 --> 00:23:21,922
Overall, the maternal mortality rate is higher in women aged 35 and older .

167
00:23:21,922 --> 00:23:31,422
Creanga and coworkers (2017) analyzed pregnancy-related deaths in the United States for 2011 through 2013.

168
00:23:31,422 --> 00:23:40,909
Although women older than 35 years contributed less than 15 percent of all live births, they constituted 31 percent of maternal deaths.

169
00:23:40,909 --> 00:23:46,559
For the fetus, maternal age-related risks primarily originate from (1)

170
00:23:46,708 --> 00:24:07,333
indicated preterm delivery for maternal complications such as hypertension and diabetes, (2) spontaneous preterm birth, (3) fetal-growth disorders related to chronic maternal disease or multifetal gestation, (4) fetal aneuploidy, and (5) pregnancies resulting from assisted reproductive technology.

171
00:24:07,333 --> 00:24:13,133
Assisted Reproductive Technologies Recall that older women have subfertility problems.

172
00:24:13,133 --> 00:24:26,908
And, although the incidence of dizygotic twinning rises with maternal age, the more important cause of multifetal gestation in older women follows the use of assisted reproductive technology (ART) and ovulation induction.

173
00:24:26,908 --> 00:24:36,833
Indeed, according to the CDC, 30 to 40 percent of all multifetal gestations in the United States in 2012 were conceived with the use of ART .

174
00:24:36,833 --> 00:24:46,470
Morbidity and mortality with multifetal pregnancies stem from preterm delivery and placentation complications, such as placenta previa and abruption .

175
00:24:46,470 --> 00:24:50,558
With ART, transmission of infectious agents is a risk.

176
00:24:50,558 --> 00:25:02,008
For women without human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection who plan conception with their HIV-affected partners, the CDC has published prevention strategies .

177
00:25:02,008 --> 00:25:09,145
Also, Zika virus infection acquisition through in vitro fertilization (IVF) has been described .

178
00:25:09,145 --> 00:25:14,420
Last, some data links ART to higher major congenital malformation rates.

179
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:29,745
In a registry of 308,974 births, 8.3 percent of neonates conceived by ART had major birth defects, and intracytoplasmic injection was also associated with an elevated risk for malformations .

180
00:25:29,745 --> 00:25:32,170
# Paternal Age.

181
00:25:32,170 --> 00:25:37,408
Advanced paternal age has doubled in the United States over the past generation.

182
00:25:37,408 --> 00:25:43,545
It is associated with a higher risk of preterm birth and new autosomal-dominant mutations .

183
00:25:43,545 --> 00:25:50,545
One example is the possible link between increasing paternal age and complex neuropsychiatric conditions .

184
00:25:50,545 --> 00:25:59,108
Parental history and experiences can also exert effects on progeny through epigenomic information not contained in the DNA sequence.

185
00:25:59,108 --> 00:26:09,395
Variations in sperm and oocyte cytosine methylation and noncoding RNAs are examples (Cedars, 2015; Lane, 2014).

186
00:26:09,395 --> 00:26:11,333
Social History.

187
00:26:11,333 --> 00:26:14,583
# Recreational Drugs and Smoking.

188
00:26:14,583 --> 00:26:26,508
Fetal risks associated with alcohol, marijuana, cocaine, amphetamines, and heroin are discussed in Chapters 8 (p. 148) and 64 (p. 1150).

189
00:26:26,508 --> 00:26:32,583
The first step in preventing drug-related fetal risk is an honest assessment of use by the patient .

190
00:26:32,583 --> 00:26:36,420
Toward this end, questioning should be nonjudgmental.

191
00:26:36,420 --> 00:26:40,370
Several validated tools screen for at-risk drinking.

192
00:26:40,370 --> 00:26:43,395
One is the wellstudied TACE questions .

193
00:26:43,395 --> 00:26:54,158
These four questions investigate having a tolerance to alcohol, being annoyed by comments about their drinking, attempting to cut down, and drinking early in the morning—the eye opener.

194
00:26:54,158 --> 00:27:03,208
Of more than 1000 postpartum patients in one Canadian study, a high percentage reported alcohol use concurrent with conception attempts .

195
00:27:03,208 --> 00:27:13,145
Specifically, nearly half of those planning for pregnancy consumed a mean of 2.2 drinks daily during early gestation and before they recognized their pregnancy.

196
00:27:13,145 --> 00:27:19,158
This frequency and pattern clearly underscore the opportunity for preconceptional counseling.

197
00:27:19,158 --> 00:27:28,583
In 2014 to 2015, 17 percent of reproductive-age women in the United States smoked cigarettes according to the CDC .

198
00:27:28,583 --> 00:27:34,708
Smoking in pregnancy is consistently associated with numerous adverse perinatal outcomes .

199
00:27:34,708 --> 00:27:44,095
These risks are largely mitigated by cessation before pregnancy, highlighting the importance of screening for tobacco use prior to and during pregnancy .

200
00:27:44,095 --> 00:27:47,095
# Environmental Exposures.

201
00:27:47,095 --> 00:27:56,333
Only a few environmental agents have been shown to cause adverse pregnancy outcomes (McCue, 2019; Windham, 2008).

202
00:27:56,333 --> 00:28:02,808
For example, contact with some chemicals may impart significant maternal and fetal risks.

203
00:28:02,808 --> 00:28:12,070
Discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 153), excess exposure to methyl mercury or lead is associated with neurodevelopmental disorders.

204
00:28:12,070 --> 00:28:24,395
For lead, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) endorses guidelines that recommend blood lead testing only if a lead-exposure risk factor is identified.

205
00:28:24,395 --> 00:28:31,133
If the levels are >5 µg/dL, counseling is completed, and the lead source is sought and removed.

206
00:28:31,133 --> 00:28:36,658
Blood lead levels >45 µg/dL are consistent with lead

207
00:28:36,820 --> 00:28:41,795
poisoning, and women in this group may be candidates for chelation therapy .

208
00:28:41,745 --> 00:28:48,795
In contrast, everyday exposures to electromagnetic fields are not linked to adverse fetal outcomes .

209
00:28:48,795 --> 00:28:57,295
Examples include energy emanated by high-voltage power lines, electric blankets, microwave ovens, and cellular phones.

210
00:28:57,295 --> 00:28:59,320
# Diet.

211
00:28:59,320 --> 00:29:06,545
Pica is the craving for and consuming of ice, laundry starch, clay, dirt, or other nonfood items.

212
00:29:06,545 --> 00:29:12,882
It should be discouraged due to its inherent replacement of healthful food with nutritionally empty products .

213
00:29:12,882 --> 00:29:19,382
In some cases, it may represent an unusual physiological response to iron deficiency .

214
00:29:19,382 --> 00:29:26,070
Many vegetarian diets are protein deficient but can be corrected by increasing egg and cheese consumption.

215
00:29:26,070 --> 00:29:36,507
Anorexia and bulimia raise maternal risks of nutritional deficiencies, electrolyte disturbances, cardiac arrhythmias, and gastrointestinal pathology .

216
00:29:36,507 --> 00:29:51,907
Discussed in Chapter 64 (p. 1149), pregnancy-related complications with these eating disorders include greater risks of low birthweight, smaller head circumference, microcephaly, and small-for-gestational-age newborns.

217
00:29:51,907 --> 00:29:55,845
Obesity is linked with several maternal complications.

218
00:29:55,845 --> 00:30:09,157
Discussed in Chapter 51 (p. 905), these include hypertension, preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, labor abnormalities, cesarean delivery, and operative complications .

219
00:30:09,157 --> 00:30:14,670
Obesity also appears to be associated with a range of structural fetal anomalies .

220
00:30:14,670 --> 00:30:16,982
# Exercise.

221
00:30:16,982 --> 00:30:22,257
Conditioned pregnant women usually can continue to exercise throughout gestation .

222
00:30:22,257 --> 00:30:31,270
Discussed in Chapter 10, covering prenatal care (p. 187), no data suggest that exercise is harmful during pregnancy.

223
00:30:31,270 --> 00:30:38,995
However, as pregnancy progresses, balance problems and joint relaxation may predispose to orthopedic injury.

224
00:30:38,995 --> 00:30:46,657
During exercise, gravidas should avoid exhaustion, overheating, dehydration, and prolonged supine position.

225
00:30:46,657 --> 00:30:49,570
# Intimate-Partner Violence.

226
00:30:49,570 --> 00:30:56,120
Pregnancy can exacerbate interpersonal problems and is a time of elevated risk from an abusive partner.

227
00:30:56,120 --> 00:31:06,907
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2012), approximately 324,000 pregnant women are abused each year.

228
00:31:06,907 --> 00:31:21,120
Intimate-partner violence (IPV) is associated with greater risk for several pregnancy-related complications that include hypertension, vaginal bleeding, hyperemesis, preterm delivery, and lowbirthweight neonates .

229
00:31:21,120 --> 00:31:31,257
Because IPV can escalate during pregnancy, even to the point of homicide, the preconceptional period provides an ideal time for screening and intervention .

230
00:31:31,257 --> 00:31:43,595
In support, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019c) provides recommendations and resources for screening both pregnant and nonpregnant women for IPV.

231
00:31:43,595 --> 00:31:48,820
# Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Transgender, and Queer.

232
00:31:48,820 --> 00:31:57,332
Individuals Preconceptional care has traditionally been based on the assumption of heterosexuality and has often excluded lesbian women .

233
00:31:57,332 --> 00:32:07,482
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018c) endorses quality health care for all women regardless of sexual orientation.

234
00:32:07,482 --> 00:32:16,270
Indeed, in a study by Carpinello and colleagues (2016), three fourths of lesbian couples planned for one partner to conceive.

235
00:32:16,270 --> 00:32:27,557
Paradoxically, adolescent bisexual and lesbian women are at greater risk for undesired pregnancies (Charlton, 2018; Hodson, 2017).

236
00:32:27,557 --> 00:32:44,620
Of special risks in this group, lesbian and bisexual women have higher incidences of obesity, tobacco and alcohol use, depression, diabetes, and low parity compared with heterosexual women (Mravcak, 2006; O’Hanlan, 2007).

237
00:32:44,620 --> 00:32:49,245
In some of these groups, knowledge of surrogacy laws is imperative .

238
00:32:49,245 --> 00:32:51,307
Screening Tests.

239
00:32:51,307 --> 00:32:59,232
Highlighted in Table 9-4, women with certain chronic medical diseases ideally would be evaluated before conception.

240
00:32:59,232 --> 00:33:04,920
With several of these, optimizing maternal condition will improve pregnancy outcomes.

241
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:07,257
Table 9-4.

242
00:33:07,257 --> 00:33:10,795
Selected Preconceptional Counseling Topics

